  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Employers and clients pay for customers and revenue they can trace to marketing work. They judge you by measured results and clear thinking.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is describing a campaign through the five funnel stages with numbers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and MBA marketing: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools draft reports, summaries and outreach. Routine output becomes common quickly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judgment, relationships and accountability hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI for drafts; later, own the decisions and the people.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general MBA marketing path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>MBA finance route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Numbers focus</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Different skills</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Digital-only route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Faster entry</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Narrower</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Product management route</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Closer to building</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Different skills</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn MBA marketing cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    MBA marketing is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free YouTube lessons and public company reports.</li>
    <li>Free NPTEL and SWAYAM management and statistics courses.</li>
    <li>Free spreadsheet practice with public data.</li>
    <li>A professional in the role who reviews your sample.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    In four weeks, run one tiny test campaign, record the funnel numbers and ask a marketer to review.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

